course: dictionary
lesson: 13549
title: Probability words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Improbable means not likely to happen or not likely to be true. Imagine a line: at one end is impossible, at the other end is certain. Improbable sits close to the impossible end, but it is not quite impossible.
explain: Stories use improbable to surprise you. «An improbable friendship grew between a cat and a duck.» Cats and ducks are not usually friends, so the words tell you the friendship is strange and surprising.
explain: Look at the start of the word. The prefix im- means not, just like in impossible. So improbable means not probable. A good check is to swap in the word unlikely: «an unlikely friendship» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Improbable means not likely to happen or to be true.
fact: Probability words run from impossible to certain, and improbable is close to impossible.
fact: «Improbably» is the adverb: «The door improbably opened by itself.»
fact: «Improbability» is the noun: «The improbability of the win made everyone gasp.»
fact: Useful phrases are «highly improbable», «it is improbable that», and «an improbable winner».
exercise: «It is improbable that it will snow in July.» What does improbable mean here? | Certain to happen | Very loud | *Not likely to happen | Happy and bright
exercise: «The improbable winner of the race was a snail.» What does improbable suggest? | *A surprising winner nobody expected | A tired runner | A fast winner everyone expected | A hungry runner
exercise: «Sam said it was improbable that the cat would be friends with the dog.» Find and copy one word that means not likely. | happy | because | *improbable | friends
test: «Highly improbable» means the thing is very what? | Certain to happen | Sweet and warm | Very loud | *Unlikely to happen
test: Which phrase means something is not likely to happen? | it is always that | it is certain that | it is definitely true that | *it is improbable that
exam: Is improbable closer to impossible or to certain? | Closer to certain | Exactly in the middle | Exactly certain | *Closer to impossible
